# Install Obot Sentry on macOS

Manually install `obot-sentry` to run one-off scans or install hooks
yourself.

1. Open Terminal and install the binary:

   ```sh
   sudo install -m 755 obot-sentry /usr/local/bin/obot-sentry
   ```

2. Configure Obot Sentry by writing the same preferences domain an MDM
   profile delivers, replacing `REPLACE_WITH_ENROLLMENT_KEY` with your
   enrollment key:

   ```sh
   sudo defaults write /Library/Preferences/com.obot.obot-sentry ServerURL -string "{{.serverURL}}"
   sudo defaults write /Library/Preferences/com.obot.obot-sentry EnrollmentKey -string "REPLACE_WITH_ENROLLMENT_KEY"
   sudo defaults write /Library/Preferences/com.obot.obot-sentry ScanIntervalMinutes -string "{{.scanIntervalMinutes}}"
   sudo defaults write /Library/Preferences/com.obot.obot-sentry EnforcementEnabled -bool {{if .enforcementEnabled}}true{{else}}false{{end}}
   ```

3. Enroll the device:

   ```sh
   sudo obot-sentry enroll
   ```

   Note: the device must be enrolled before submitting scans or
   installing hooks.

## Run a scan

```sh
obot-sentry scan --submit
```

By default, scans are submitted at most once every {{.scanIntervalMinutes}} minutes; runs within that window skip submission.

## Install hooks

```sh
sudo obot-sentry hook-install{{if .enforcementEnabled}} --enforce{{end}}
```
{{if .enforcementEnabled}}
Enforcement is on for this configuration, so `hook-install` also installs the
pre-tool hooks that check every tool call against Obot's allowlist. Read
[Tool-call enforcement](#tool-call-enforcement) before running it: from that
point on, tool calls this device cannot get a verdict for are blocked.

## Tool-call enforcement

Each tool call in Claude Code, Codex, and Cursor is checked against Obot's
allowlist before it runs. The check is a single request to Obot, and it is
**fail-closed**: if Obot cannot be reached, the device is not enrolled, or the
hook cannot tell which MCP server a call targets, the call is blocked.

- Blocking a call never *approves* one. Your agents' own approval prompts still
  apply to everything that is allowed.
- Visual Studio Code is not supported for enforcement. Its audit hook is unaffected.
- An MCP server can be allowlisted by URL, by its `npx`/`uvx` package, or — for a
  claude.ai connector — by display name. A stdio server started from a local
  executable path presents none of those, so it cannot be allowlisted and is
  blocked by design.

To see what this machine reports for a server, and why:

```sh
obot-sentry enforce resolve --agent claude-code --server <server-name>
```

Turning enforcement off in Obot stops the blocking immediately — the server
allows every call when enforcement is disabled — even before the hooks
themselves are removed.
{{end}}
## Remove the hooks

`hook-install` has no uninstall command, so its hook configuration must be
removed by hand. Every entry it writes is marked with `--managed-by
obot-sentry`. Find the files that still carry one:

```sh
grep -rl 'managed-by obot-sentry' \
  "$HOME/.claude" "$HOME/.copilot" \
  "$HOME/Library/Application Support/Code/User" \
  /etc/codex "/Library/Application Support/Cursor" 2>/dev/null
```

Then clean up each agent:

- **Copilot (VS Code) hook** — this file is written only by Obot Sentry, so
  delete it:

  ```sh
  rm -f "$HOME/.copilot/hooks/obot-sentry.json"
  ```

- **Claude Code** (`~/.claude/settings.json`), **Codex**
  (`/etc/codex/requirements.toml`, edit with `sudo`), and **Cursor**
  (`/Library/Application Support/Cursor/hooks.json`, edit with `sudo`) — open
  each file and delete every hook entry whose command contains `--managed-by
  obot-sentry`, leaving any of your own hooks in place. In the Codex file Obot
  Sentry also pins two values under `[features]`: `hooks = true`, which you can
  drop if Obot Sentry was the only reason hooks were enabled, and
  `non_prefixed_mcp_tool_names = false`, which you can drop if you want that
  Codex feature available again.

- **VS Code settings** (`~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/settings.json`)
  — under `chat.hookFilesLocations`, remove the keys Obot Sentry added
  (`~/.copilot/hooks`, `.claude/settings.json`, `.claude/settings.local.json`,
  `~/.claude/settings.json`); delete the whole `chat.hookFilesLocations` object
  if you had no other entries in it.

The per-user files (`~/.claude`, `~/.copilot`, VS Code settings) exist once per
signed-in user, so repeat for each user on a shared Mac. Restart Claude Code,
Codex, VS Code, and Cursor afterward to drop the hooks.

## Uninstall

Remove the hooks (above), then remove the binary, the configuration, and the
device identity and scan state:

```sh
sudo rm -f /usr/local/bin/obot-sentry
sudo defaults delete /Library/Preferences/com.obot.obot-sentry
sudo rm -rf "/Library/Application Support/obot/obot-sentry"
rm -rf "$HOME/Library/Application Support/obot/obot-sentry" "$HOME/Library/Caches/obot/obot-sentry"
```
